From: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mmap: keep EPERM for MAP_LOCKED

mmap turns EPERM from the kernel into ENOMEM for an anonymous mapping,
for kernels that reported an overcommit limit so.  A MAP_LOCKED mapping
without a memory-lock limit or CAP_IPC_LOCK fails with EPERM, as Linux
documents it, and that one is right.

Signed-off-by: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>

diff --git a/src/mman/mmap.c b/src/mman/mmap.c
index 43e5e02..de0f73b 100644
--- a/src/mman/mmap.c
+++ b/src/mman/mmap.c
@@ -30,8 +30,10 @@ void *__mmap(void *start, size_t len, int prot, int flags, int fd, off_t off)
 #else
 	ret = __syscall(SYS_mmap, start, len, prot, flags, fd, off);
 #endif
-	/* Fixup incorrect EPERM from kernel. */
-	if (ret == -EPERM && !start && (flags&MAP_ANON) && !(flags&MAP_FIXED))
+	/* Fixup incorrect EPERM from kernel. With MAP_LOCKED it is the
+	 * memory-lock limit's, and right. */
+	if (ret == -EPERM && !start && (flags&MAP_ANON) && !(flags&MAP_FIXED)
+	    && !(flags&MAP_LOCKED))
 		ret = -ENOMEM;
 	return (void *)__syscall_ret(ret);
 }
